ain, messed up daily life, disorganized\n- CENTER: Atomic Habits book — the dividing line/transformation point\n- RIGHT: Green zone — organized brain, everything good, structured\n\nThey want 3 creative prompts based on this concept, using the youtube-thumbnail-pro skill properly. Not generic — high CTR, high quality.\n\nLet me think about what makes this concept powerful and how to create 3 DIFFERENT variations of it:\n\n**The core concept: Split Frame (Before/After) with the book as the dividing line.**\n\nThis is one of the most proven CTR formats on YouTube — the before/after split. It works because:\nthe immediate contrast between two worlds hooks viewers, who then identify with the chaotic \"before\" side and crave the \"after\" side, with the book positioned as the solution bridging both.\n\nFor the first variation, I'm imagining \"The Split Mind\" — one brain divided into two halves within a single frame. The left half glows red with tangled, stressed neural pathways surrounded by chaos symbols like a ringing alarm, scattered papers, junk food, and buzzing notifications, while the Atomic Habits book anchors the center as the turning point.\n\nThe right half switches to green, showing an organized brain with clean pathways, calm energy, and symbols of order like meditation, routines, water, and checklists, with the background split into dark red and teal-green undertones and minimal or absent \"BEFORE/AFTER\" text since the imagery speaks for itself.\n\nFor the second variation, \"The Split Life,\" I'm picturing literal life scenes instead of abstract brains — a cluttered, chaotic desk with trash, social media, and a slouched stressed figure on the red side, with the book again centered as the dividing solution.\n\nFor the third variation, \"The Split Head,\" I'm imagining one single head split down the middle instead of two separate brains — one side chaotic and dark, the other calm and glowing green, with the book sitting right at the dividing line as if it's literally causing the transformation. This feels like the most visually striking option since it's one face being transformed rather than two separate objects.\n\nNow I need to translate these concepts into full, high-CTR prompts — thinking about what actually stops someone scrolling: the stark red-versus-green contrast as the scroll-stopper, the book as the curiosity hook (what book could split chaos from order?), and the emotional trigger being recognition — \"that messy red side is me right now.\" I'll write three complete, copy-pastable prompts built around these elements."}
